      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Case 2 m > 1: Since policy π∗produces the minimum cumulative discounted cost for a given state s,",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        112.84063720703125,
        505.24481201171875,
        123.83893585205078
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the core of this proof will be demonstrating that the minimum cumulative discounted cost of entering",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        125.84136962890625,
        504.0002746582031,
        134.7479248046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the feasible set (call this value HE) is less than the minimum cumulative discounted cost of not",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        136.59095764160156,
        503.9998474121094,
        146.35194396972656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "entering the feasible set (call this value HN), and therefore π∗will choose the route of entering the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        145.56866455078125,
        503.9963073730469,
        157.2609405517578
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "feasible set.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        158.568359375,
        155.033447265625,
        167.47491455078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The proof will proceed by deriving a sufficient condition for HE < HN by establishing bounds on",
      "bbox": [
        107.69100189208984,
        174.7979278564453,
        504.00054931640625,
        185.35791015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "them.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        185.8663330078125,
        130.4158477783203,
        194.77288818359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We place an upper bound on the minimum cumulative discounted cost of entering the feasible set",
      "bbox": [
        107.53199768066406,
        202.25433349609375,
        503.99688720703125,
        211.160888671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "HE. Since ∃π that enters the feasible set in m −1 steps, entering the feasible set can be at most the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        212.44607543945312,
        503.9969177246094,
        222.7659454345703
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "highest possible cost that π incurs. Since the maximum cost at any state is Hmax, the upper bound is",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        223.91297912597656,
        503.9964599609375,
        233.67494201660156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the discounted sum of m −1 steps of violations Hmax, or",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        234.26504516601562,
        338.3661804199219,
        244.5839385986328
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "HE < Hmax(1 −γm−1)",
      "bbox": [
        254.72402954101562,
        252.03070068359375,
        356.0815734863281,
        271.04376220703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(1 −γ)",
      "bbox": [
        304.8389892578125,
        266.9780578613281,
        335.45745849609375,
        276.38275146484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We place a lower bound on the minimum cumulative discounted cost of not entering the feasible",
      "bbox": [
        107.53199768066406,
        291.2933654785156,
        503.9967346191406,
        300.1999206542969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "set HN. In this case, say in the sampled trajectory, the maximum gap between any two non-zero",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        302.04296875,
        503.9951171875,
        311.8049011230469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "violations is w. By definition, the trajectory cannot have an infinite sequence of violation-free states",
      "bbox": [
        107.7509994506836,
        312.95196533203125,
        504.0033264160156,
        322.0179138183594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "since the trajectory never enters the feasible set. Therefore w is finite. Now recall Hmin is the lower",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        323.8619689941406,
        504.170166015625,
        334.4219055175781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "bound on the non-zero values of h. So the minimum cumulative discounted cost of not entering the",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        334.7709655761719,
        504.00360107421875,
        343.8369140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "feasible set must be at least the cost of the trajectory with a violation of Hmin at intervals of w steps.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        345.6799621582031,
        505.7434997558594,
        356.2398986816406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "That is:",
      "bbox": [
        107.69100189208984,
        356.74835205078125,
        137.85777282714844,
        365.6549072265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Hmin(γw)",
      "bbox": [
        270.4949951171875,
        364.9701843261719,
        312.3854675292969,
        376.27288818359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(1 −γw) < HN",
      "bbox": [
        272.906005859375,
        373.2509460449219,
        341.456298828125,
        388.9317321777344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Now HE < HN will be true if the upper bound of HE is less than the lower bound of HN. In other",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        401.1999816894531,
        504.1669921875,
        411.7599182128906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "words HE < HN is true if:",
      "bbox": [
        107.64099884033203,
        412.1089782714844,
        216.97998046875,
        422.6689147949219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Hmax(1 −γm−1)",
      "bbox": [
        241.28500366210938,
        437.02667236328125,
        313.1514587402344,
        448.71990966796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(1 −γ)",
      "bbox": [
        261.90899658203125,
        451.97406005859375,
        292.5284729003906,
        461.3787536621094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "< Hmin(γw)",
      "bbox": [
        317.1130065917969,
        437.4172058105469,
        370.7154846191406,
        454.54473876953125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(1 −γw)",
      "bbox": [
        331.2359924316406,
        451.72821044921875,
        368.3044738769531,
        461.3787536621094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(5)",
      "bbox": [
        493.051025390625,
        445.85736083984375,
        504.6673889160156,
        454.763916015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Rearranging, we get:",
      "bbox": [
        108.00003051757812,
        468.2723693847656,
        191.2773895263672,
        477.1789245605469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Hmax",
      "bbox": [
        240.09902954101562,
        480.0939636230469,
        263.0887451171875,
        489.8548889160156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Hmin",
      "bbox": [
        240.85000610351562,
        493.6669616699219,
        262.3324279785156,
        503.42889404296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "<",
      "bbox": [
        267.5429992675781,
        486.83294677734375,
        275.2939147949219,
        495.6797180175781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(1 −γ) · (γw)",
      "bbox": [
        296.7170104980469,
        478.5521545410156,
        354.4404602050781,
        488.9407043457031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(1 −γm−1) · (1 −γw)",
      "bbox": [
        279.2550048828125,
        492.47265625,
        371.9024963378906,
        502.51373291015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(6)",
      "bbox": [
        493.051025390625,
        486.9923400878906,
        504.6673889160156,
        495.8988952636719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Let’s define the RHS of the Inequality 6 as the function υ(γ). Consider γ ∈(0, 1). It is not difficult",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        514.8880615234375,
        503.99969482421875,
        524.5119018554688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to demonstrate that υ(γ) in this domain range is a continuous function and that left directional limit",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        526.35498046875,
        504.0040283203125,
        535.4209594726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "limγ→1−υ(γ) = ∞. This suggests that there is an open interval of values for γ (whose supremum",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        536.7060546875,
        503.99969482421875,
        547.4129028320312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "is 1) for which Hmax/Hmin < υ(γ) and so HE < HN. So we establish that ∃ϵ > 0 such that for",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        547.6151123046875,
        504.16851806640625,
        558.5137329101562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "γ ∈(1 −ϵ, 1), we satisfy the sufficient condition HE < HN so that the optimal policy will enter its",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        558.5240478515625,
        503.9962158203125,
        569.6419067382812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "feasible set.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        570.150390625,
        155.033447265625,
        579.0569458007812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Thus, we prove that if there is a policy entering its feasible set from state s, then there is a range of",
      "bbox": [
        107.69100189208984,
        586.3799438476562,
        504.00360107421875,
        595.4459228515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "values for γ that are close enough to 1 ensuring that the optimal policy of Main paper Equation 3 will",
      "bbox": [
        107.7509994506836,
        597.2889404296875,
        504.0014953613281,
        606.3549194335938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "enter its feasible set in a finite number of steps with minimum discounted sum of costs.",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        608.3573608398438,
        455.0173034667969,
        617.263916015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "C.4",
      "bbox": [
